22:The present study was designed to investigate the level of six heavy metals (arsenic, cadmium, cooper, iron, mercury and
23:lead) in both wild-growing and packaged samples from twelve commonly used medicinal plants and spices in Romania. The
24:concentrations of all heavy metals, except mercury and lead, were within permissible limits in the analysed plant samples.
25-The wild-growing sample of Tilia cordata and some packaged samples of Origanum vulgare and Thymus vulgare contained
26:higher levels of mercury than 0.1 mg/kg. The concentrations of lead exceeded the limit recommended by the EU (0.3 mg/kg)
27-for some samples of Achillea millefolium, Matricaria chamomilla, Mentha piperita, Ocimum basilicum, Origanum majorana
28-and Tilia cordata. The findings of the study highlight the need for constant monitoring of heavy metals residues in order to
29-ensure the quality and safety of herbal products.
