93-Genotoxicity is a property possessed by some
94-substances that makes them harmful to the genetic
95-information, contained in organisms (Mishraet al.,
96-2013). While there are many different factors that can
97-affect DNA, RNA and other genetic materials, the
98-property of genotoxicity only applies to those
99-substances that actually cause harm to the genetic
100-information (Mishraet al., 2013). A substance that has
101-the property of genotoxicity is known as a geno-toxin.
102-Micronuclei are cytoplasmic chromatin-containing
103-bodies that appears in the cell like a small satellite
104-nucleus around the cell nucleus, due to chromosome
105-fragments or entire chromosomes that are not
106-incorporated in the main nucleus after cell division
107-(Mishraet al., 2013). The presence of micronuclei
108-(MN) in cells is considered as a biomarker of damage
109-to the DNA (Mishraet al., 2013). The micronucleus
110-test is an in vivo and in vitro short-time screening
111-cytogenetic test is a widely used method for assessing
112-genotoxicity of chemicals in organisms (Mishraet al.,
113-2013).
