12-Spices represent all edible plant parts used for food flavouring or colouring including
13-fruits, roots, seeds, peels or vegetable substances. The objective of this study was
14-to determine the elemental composition (Ag, Al, B, Ba, Bi, Ca, Cd, Co, Cr, Cu, Fe,
15:In, K, Li, Mg, Mn, Na, Ni, Pb, Sr, Tl and Zn) of selected spices available in the local
16:markets on Serbian territory. The presence of heavy metals in spices is of particular importance because this could result in the accumulation of these elements in
17-human organs, which further might cause different health problems. The analysed
18-spice samples were curcuma, star anise, cinnamon, ginger, coriander, cardamom,
19-sesame, black pepper, chilli and curry. The preparation of the samples was done
20-by wet digestion. The concentrations of elements after digestion were determined
21-by Inductively Coupled Plasma ‒ Optical Emission Spectrometry (ICP-OES). In the
22:group of spices macroelements, from all examined elements the calcium concentration was the highest (182.25 ‒ 3968.79 mg/kg), about 83%. From the heavy metals
23:group, aluminium was most present in the range of 0.0-10.78 mg/kg, while cadmium
24-and nickel were detected in almost negligible amounts.
