31-Introduction
32-Spices include fruits, roots, seeds, peels or vegetable substances [1]. The specific ways of using spices are
33-significantly different among cultures and countries. They
34-were primarily used in religious rituals, medicine, cosmetics,
35-perfumery and as the addition to the human nutrition diet.
36-The discovery of medicinal effects of spices significantly
37-increased their use in most parts of the world including Europe [2-7]. Due to antioxidant, antimicrobial, diabetic, antiinflammatory and anti-hypersensitive properties, spices are
38-used for medicinal proposes [8,9]. On the other side, spices
39-in food play an important role in health, partially as a nutrients source.
40-Mineral nutrients are interrelated and balanced against
41-each other in human physiology. For instance, calcium,
42-sodium, magnesium and phosphorus are included in the
43-body fluid regulation. Inadequate mineral intake generally produces deficiency symptoms as anemia, impaired
44-healing of wounds, severe diarrhea and a chronic renal
45-failure [10,11]. But, although spices represent a good
46-source of nutrients, sometimes they might possess toxic
47:compounds or accumulated heavy metals that originate
48-from the production or processing and storage conditions.
49-Heavy metals beyond the permissible limits could effects the
50-human health and may lead to illness of a human fetus, preterm labor and mental retardation in children, while adults
51-may suffer from fatigue, high blood pressure and kidney
52:troubles. The use of heavy metals contaminated spices in
53-
54-human nutrition could result in accumulation of these elements in human organs, which further might cause different health problems [12]. For these reasons, the interest for
55-harmful metals present in food has been increased in the
56-last two decades [13,14].
57-In the past, scientists made an effort to determine the
58-presence of macro- and microelements in spices that originated from all parts of the world. Several scientists have investigated the composition of Indian spices in term of their
59-volatile oils and vitamins contents [15,16], as well as chemical and toxic constituents. Ila and Jagam (1980) have also
60-reported about 24 certain elements in six Indian spices [17].
61-Abou-Arab and Abou-Donia (2000) have determined heavy
62-metal contaminants in Egyptian spices [18]. The methods
63-that were previously used were either expensive or did
64-not allow simultaneous estimation of the concerned micro
65-minerals. Inductively coupled plasma with optical emission
66-spectrometry (ICP-OES) is a well-established method for
67-multi elemental analysis and determination of isotope ratios. This method allows a simultaneous analysis of a wide
68-range of trace elements in the sample and has been successfully applied in this study [19]. The aim of this study
69-was to determine the elemental composition (Ag, Al, B, Ba,
70:Bi, Ca, Cd, Co, Cr, Cu, Fe, In, K, Li, Mg, Mn, Na, Ni, Pb,
71-Sr, Tl and Zn) of selected spices available in local markets
72-on Serbian territory by using an inductively coupled plasma
73-with optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES). The con-
