215:(Table 4).
216-Heavy metals belong to a special group of elements
217-classified as the most dangerous inorganic environment
218-pollutants. They are non-biodegradable with the tendency towards bioaccumulation and expression of toxic
219-effects even at a very low concentration. Some heavy
220-metals are among the group of trace microelements because even they are toxic in higher concentrations. At
221-lower concentrations they are necessary for the plant
222-and other living organisms` growth and development.
223:The accumulation of heavy metals in plants depends not
224-only on the total content in the soil, but also on the kind
225-of affinity, as well as individual and interactive effects of
226-different soil properties [31].
227:Aluminium is included as a cofactor in the guanine
228:nucleotide binding which is necessary for the metabolism of protein. The presence of aluminium in spice samples was detected at 0.0-10.78 mg/kg (Table 4). The increased content of this element in patient kidneys can
229-significantly damage the skeleton due to the negative
230-impact on the process of the bone formation, leading to
231:the occurrence of osteomalacia [32]. Other heavy metals
232-detected in no significant amounts in the spice samples
233:are Pb and Ni, while the presence of Cd was detected
234-just in Cinnamon spice in the quantity safe for the health.
